      "worked": "For $C_1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-2x+y^2-6y+6&=0\\\\(x-1)^2-1+(y-3)^2-9+6&=0\\\\(x-1)^2+(y-3)^2&=4\\end{align*}$$\nFor $C_2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-10x+y^2-6y+25&=0\\\\(x-5)^2-25+(y-3)^2-9+25&=0\\\\(x-5)^2+(y-3)^2&=9\\end{align*}$$\nThe centres are $(1,3)$ and $(5,3)$, with radii $2$ and $3$, respectively. Both centres have the same $y$-coordinate, so their distance is $d=4$.\n\nFor positive radii and distinct centres, $d=|r_1-r_2|$ gives internal tangency; a smaller distance leaves one circle inside the other without touching. Also, $d=r_1+r_2$ gives external tangency; a larger distance separates the circles. Between these boundaries, $|r_1-r_2|<d<r_1+r_2$, the circles intersect twice.\n\nHere, $|3-2|<4<3+2$, or $1<4<5$. Therefore, there are $2$ intersection points.",
